```
This is the classic geraintluff sha256 — ASCII-only. My input is a hex-ish string (formId + JSON) which may contain non-ASCII (notes in fields!). Non-ASCII would break it. I'll pre-encode to UTF-8 bytes then hex, feed hex string (ASCII) through: `toUtf8Bytes(text)` then map to two hex chars each. Then hex→ASCII is fine. Actually simpler: build the byte array directly and process bytes instead of chars. The classic implementation works on a JS string charCode; feeding it an ASCII hex string of the UTF-8 encoding is clean:

```js
function utf8Hex(text) {
  var bytes = new TextEncoder().encode(text);  // browser + node both have TextEncoder
  var hex = "";
  for (var i = 0; i < bytes.length; i++) hex += bytes[i].toString(16).padStart(2, "0");
  return hex;
}
function sha256Hex(text) {
  var encoded = utf8Hex(text);
  if (root.crypto && root.crypto.subtle && root.crypto.subtle.digest) {
    return root.crypto.subtle.digest("SHA-256", new TextEncoder().encode(text)).then(...)
  }
  return Promise.resolve(sha256Fallback(encoded));
}
```
Hmm — async subtle vs sync fallback: both return promises. Fine. But subtle in non-secure contexts (http on LAN IP) is undefined → fallback used. In Node, globalThis.crypto.subtle exists → subtle path used by tests; fallback tested with known vectors separately. Good — both paths covered.

Wait — crypto.subtle.digest requires secure context in browsers; Node: globalThis.crypto available since 19 (webcrypto). Tests run in Node → subtle path. But I should unit-test the fallback too: sha256Fallback via known vectors (I'll compute expected hex in Python hashlib and assert — cross-language pinning). And deriveKey: test in Node that deriveKey("pay-form", {...}) equals Python hashlib.sha256(b'pay-form|{...}').hexdigest() for the same JSON string — I'll construct the exact JSON string in the JS test and compare against a fixture computed in Python. Since JSON.stringify({"to_handle":"bob","amount":"15.00"}) = '{"to_handle":"bob","amount":"15.00"}' (no spaces) — Python json.dumps with separators=(",", ":") gives the same. I'll pin a couple of vectors.

